    "accordion_X9KDBB": {
      "type": "accordion",
      "blocks": {
        "text_8HeDjV": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is Washington DC tap water safe to drink? ",
            "answer": "<p>DC water is technically federal compliant; however, it also contains 37 detected contaminants, including 13 that are above independent health guidelines. While it is 'legal', that does necessarily translate into it being 'safe'.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_QbTBtT": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can you drink DC tap water directly from the tap? ",
            "answer": "<p>Yes, but there are risks. Tap water quality in Washington varies depending on neighbourhood, age of pipes and location of lead service lines, so filtration is a strongly recommended precaution.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_XbcVQV": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How serious is Washington water contamination from lead? ",
            "answer": "<p>Very serious. Lead contamination in Washington water is due to the countless numbers of aged pipes. These pipes are still being used. There is not a safe level of lead exposure, particularly among children and pregnant mothers.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_NXHddK": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is the current state of Washington contaminated water from PFAS? ",
            "answer": "<p><strong>PFAS in Washington water</strong> has been found in a variety of water sources around Washington, including the Potomac River and industrially contaminated sites. Known as \"forever chemicals,\" PFAS do not degrade and steadily build up in the body over time.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_yUeHba": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What makes a water filter truly effective for Washington homes?",
            "answer": "<p>This level of contamination is, in general, more difficult and much more complex in nature. Lead, oxides from corroding pipes, PFAS from industrial run-off, TTHMs from chlorination, Atrazine from agricultural run-off ,all require different filtration methods. <strong>washington water filter systems</strong> are not one size fits all; rather, they should be created to meet the needs of this specific combination or elimination of up to 99.9% of the particular contaminants.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_fE9Y4D": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What are the best Washington water filter systems for everyday use? ",
            "answer": "<p><strong>Washington water filter systems</strong> include pitchers designed for use on your countertop and those installed underneath the sink. If you want to be fully protected from lead, PFAS, and disinfection byproducts then a certified multi-stage system like Phoenix Gravity Water Filter is the best.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_XzRedf": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need a whole house water filter in Washington? ",
            "answer": "<p>If you own an older property or where you live is in a danger zone of contamination, installation of a <strong>whole house water filter in Washington</strong> can guarantee that all water in your house is free from the highest level of impurities.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_bVMT6E": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why choose a stainless steel water filter Washington? ",
            "answer": "<p>The durable construction of a <strong>stainless steel water filter in Washington</strong> is not only long-lasting and resistant to pathogens but also offers a great alternative to conventional plastic filter housings as they will not leach toxic chemicals into your drinking water.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
